From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mm/nommu: fail private mappings larger than the largest page block

do_mmap_private() allocates the memory for a private mapping with
alloc_pages_exact(), which allocates one block of order get_order(len).
The largest order of the page allocator is MAX_PAGE_ORDER, and a request
for a larger order fails and prints a WARNING with a stack trace the
first time it happens. Any unprivileged program reaches it with one
large mmap() or malloc():

  WARNING: mm/page_alloc.c:5394 at __alloc_frozen_pages_noprof+0x2c8/0x862
  ...
  do_mmap+0x550/0x73c
  vm_mmap_pgoff+0x88/0xc0

Check the order first and fail such a mapping through the existing
allocation-failure path, which returns -ENOMEM and prints the usual
"Allocation of length ... failed" report.

Reproduced on QEMU's riscv64 virt machine with nommu_virt_defconfig: an
8 MiB MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS mmap() fails with -ENOMEM either way,
and the WARNING only appears without this patch.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>

---
--- a/mm/nommu.c
+++ b/mm/nommu.c
@@ -967,6 +967,9 @@ static int do_mmap_private(struct vm_area_struct *vma,
 	 *   we're allocating is smaller than a page
 	 */
 	order = get_order(len);
+	/* alloc_pages_exact() fails, with a WARN, above MAX_PAGE_ORDER. */
+	if (order > MAX_PAGE_ORDER)
+		goto enomem;
 	total = 1 << order;
 	point = len >> PAGE_SHIFT;
 
